Timing-based side channels have been widely used to infer sensitive properties of neural network inference, with prior work demonstrating class-dependent timing leakage in CPU-based deployments. As modern machine-learning systems increasingly rely on CPUs, it remains unclear whether such timing-based leakage persists under GPU execution. In this work, we re-evaluate timing side channels on GPUs across multiple convolutional neural network architectures using the CIFAR-10 dataset. Our results show that, unlike CPUs, end-to-end inference latency on GPUs is largely insensitive to input class, with statistical tests indicating no significant class-level distinguishability based on timing alone. We attribute this behavior to differences in GPU execution, including SIMT execution and predicated control flow. Importantly, kernel-level profiling provides preliminary evidence that class-dependent execution differences persist at finer granularity, observable through instruction-level performance counters. These findings highlight that timing alone is insufficient for characterizing inference-time side-channel behavior on GPUs and motivate a re-examination of threat models developed for CPU-based inference.